<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maria Temryukovna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1544–<a href="/years/1569">1569</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maria Temryukovna was a Kabardian princess, the daughter of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temryuk-idarov">Temryuk of Kabarda</a>, who was baptised and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV of Russia</a> in 1561 as his second wife. The marriage sealed Kabarda's alliance with Muscovy and brought Kabardian princely families into the Russian nobility. She died in 1569, and her brother Mikhail Cherkassky rose high at the Muscovite court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maria-temryukovna.jpg" alt="Maria Temryukovna" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shahiiiyhh · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IMG_mahidevran_sultan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maria was born Kucheney, the daughter of the Eastern Circassian Prince Temryuk of Kabardia. After the death of Ivan the Terrible's first wife, Anastasia Romanovna, she was presented to the tsar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and the marriage followed the collapse of his negotiations to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-jagiellon">Catherine Jagiellon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Ivan was so struck by her beauty that he resolved to marry her at once. She was baptised before the wedding, taking the name Maria, and the marriage took place on 21 August 1561, four days before Ivan's 31st birthday, when she was about 15.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As tsaritsa of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> she had a son, Vasili, named after her father-in-law, who was born on 21 March 1563 and died on 3 May the same year. Some contemporaries described her as cruel, illiterate and vindictive, unable to adapt fully to Muscovite life and a poor stepmother to Ivan's sons Ivan and Feodor, and some sources claim that she first urged her husband to create the oprichniki. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-isabel-island">Isabel</a> de Madariaga, however, notes that most historians reject the idea that she or any of his other wives had influence over Ivan. In the summer of 1564 she went with him on a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a>, where monks and other observers were impressed by her modest bearing, piety and grasp of affairs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Maria died on 1 September 1569, in her early twenties. Rumours spread that her husband had poisoned her, but there is no historical evidence for them. She appears as Marie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-bizet">Georges Bizet</a>'s opera <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a>, in the Soviet silent film The Wings of a Serf of 1926, in Pavel Lungin's film <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> of 2009 and in the Russian miniseries The Terrible of 2020, and Heinz Konsalik told her story with Ivan's in his book Der Leibarzt der Zarin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maria Temryukovna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kabarda’s alliance with Muscovy brought <b>Maria Temryukovna</b>, daughter of Prince Temryuk of Kabarda, to Moscow as the second wife of Ivan the Terrible in 1561, and Kabardian princely families entered the Russian nobility.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kabardia. Died in Alexandrov, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.4,38.74&amp;year=1560" aria-label="Alexandrov on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Maria Temryukovna"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5412 5281h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5412 5281h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=56.4,38.74&amp;year=1560">What was here in 1560? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
